sections.s2_technical:
`ThreadFactory` has exactly one method. You implement `newThread`, build the Thread yourself, configure it, and return it. For example, to give every thread a readable name you keep an `AtomicInteger` counter and name each one `prefix + "-thread-" + counter.incrementAndGet()`, so the first settlement worker becomes `settlement-pool-thread-1`.

`thread.setDaemon(true)` marks the thread as a daemon so the JVM can exit without waiting for it. For a bank batch job that must finish you keep threads non-daemon; for a background cache-refresh pool you set daemon true so a deploy shutdown is not blocked.

`thread.setPriority(Thread.NORM_PRIORITY)` sets priority from 1 to 10, though most OS schedulers treat this as a weak hint, so you rarely rely on it. You might nudge a fraud-scoring pool slightly higher than a reporting pool: `thread.setPriority(Thread.NORM_PRIORITY + 1)`.

`thread.setUncaughtExceptionHandler((t, e) -> log.severe(...))` catches any throwable that escapes the `run` method. Inside an ExecutorService, exceptions from `submit` are captured in the Future, but exceptions from `execute` or from the factory path itself would otherwise print to stderr and vanish; the handler guarantees they are logged with the thread name.

You pass the factory to the pool: `new ThreadPoolExecutor(core, max, keepAlive, unit, queue, myFactory)` or the simpler `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8, myFactory)`. From that moment every worker the pool spins up is created through your factory.

```java
ThreadFactory factory = r -> {
    Thread t = new Thread(r, "settlement-pool-thread-" + COUNTER.incrementAndGet());
    t.setDaemon(false);
    t.setPriority(Thread.NORM_PRIORITY);
    t.setUncaughtExceptionHandler((th, ex) ->
        LOG.log(Level.SEVERE, "Uncaught in " + th.getName(), ex));
    return t;
};
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4, factory);
```

A senior nuance: wrap, do not replace, the default. If you want standard behavior plus a name, delegate to `Executors.defaultThreadFactory().newThread(r)` and then rename. Also keep the counter static or per-factory-instance so names stay unique across the pool's lifetime, including after a worker dies and is replaced.

